A||N||3||Vlad||Broken bolt/stud.....? Please, advice....||||5||
Z||000000||Vlad||02-01-2001||10:33 PM||||Hello,<P>Working on the car, I noticed that the front exhaust manifold heat shield was lose. <BR>Well... Trying to fix it I locate the guilty bolt, pull it and...... it easily comes out of its hole. S@$%^&t....... I can't get what is wrong. The bolt/stud has no obvious tread damage. When I push it into the hole, it goes all the way without any resistance.<BR>I don't know....... Either  the bolt is bad, or the inner tread's gone.... But in either case, it wouldn't go in so easy. <BR>Maybe its size is wrong.... ?<BR>What should I do if I can't find the bolt that will fit?<BR>BTW, in additional to the light heat shield, there something else between manifold and  block. Haynes doesn't mention it. What did it come from?<P>Sorry for so many questions,<P>Vlad<BR>85 GT V6 Auto||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mark||02-01-2001||10:55 PM||||Vlad,<P>The stud should be M8 x 1.25 thread.  The "other thing" between the manifold and head is another heat shield.  I think this was added as part of one of the the V6 recalls.  <P>I would first try to thread a new stud or bolt into the head.  Hard to believe the internal threads in the head are gone.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Vlad||02-01-2001||11:02 PM||||Thanks <P>Great info. Size is worthless.......... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Vlad ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||02-02-2001||01:38 AM||||try a longer stud to try and reach good threads. if not use a helecoil or retap hole for a bigger one. Thes are not supposed to be all that tite in the first place. Too many people overtighten them causeing them to break when they get hot. use new studs, grade 8 or stainless too.||||reg||1
